      "conditions": ["A lifecycle phase materially affects product meaning or a requirement."],
      "exceptions": ["Not-applicable phases require a stated reason.", "A requirement may defer a phase only by naming the deferment and its later owning surface."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 676,
          "quote": "Silent absence is not a valid lifecycle answer.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#SPEC_METHOD%2F2.%20The%20chain%2FOperational%20lifecycle%20sufficiency",
          "start_line": 661
        }
      ],
      "key": "lifecycle_signal_and_gaps",
      "statement": "Product and requirements provide enough constitutional signal to derive intended lifecycle or explicitly record ambiguity; they need not prescribe every phase's mechanism or make every requirement a release plan. Product names boundary, use, release/install, live-use, observability and retirement/supersession posture where material. Requirements state invariant lifecycle, ownership, source-truth, authority and interpretation obligations. Each phase is answered, reasoned not-applicable or a named gap. Missing signal cannot be invented through local convention, implementation precedent, prompts or fixtures: reprice the owning surface or retain explicit governed debt. Phase states map to no result; an evaluation supplies its own predicate/core value, and answered/not-applicable is not satisfied.",
